# AAI 2025 — Machine Learning Homework (House Prices, Churn, Segmentation, Forecasting)

This repo contains my edited/improved versions of the four starter scripts for
the ML homework assignment. Each script was rewritten to use a **real public
dataset** (not just the tiny 10-row sample from the starter code), with an
automatic fallback to a documented synthetic dataset if a live download isn't
available in whatever environment runs the script.

## What's in `ML/`

| File | Part | Real data source |
|---|---|---|
| `house_price_prediction.py` | Part 1 — House price prediction | [Ames Housing dataset](https://jse.amstat.org/v19n3/decock/AmesHousing.txt) (2,930 real home sales, De Cock 2011) |
| `customer_churn_prediction.py` | Part 2 — Customer churn | [IBM Telco Customer Churn](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/blastchar/telco-customer-churn) (7,043 real customers) |
| `customer_segmentation.py` | Part 3 — Customer segmentation | [Mall Customer Segmentation Data](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/vjchoudhary7/customer-segmentation-tutorial-in-python) (200 real mall customers) |
| `forecasting_sales.py` | Extra Credit — Housing demand forecasting | [FRED Existing Home Sales (EXHOSLUSM495N)](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/EXHOSLUSM495N) (real monthly government data) |

Each script's docstring at the top documents:
- the exact real data source and download URL,
- how the real columns map onto the assignment's feature names,
- the fallback synthetic dataset used only if the download fails, and
- (for the extra credit script) assumptions, challenges, and potential improvements.

**Why the fallback exists:** these scripts were developed and tested in a
sandboxed environment with no general internet access, so I could not
literally exercise the live downloads there. The scripts try the real URL
first — this is what will actually run when you execute them in Google
Colab, GitHub Actions, or your own laptop, all of which have normal internet
access. If a download ever fails (offline grading machine, a dataset host
being temporarily down, etc.), the script automatically falls back to a
synthetic dataset built to match that real dataset's published statistics,
so it always runs end-to-end either way. Each run prints which path it took.

